U.S. Should Brevite Artificial Intelligence Financing
According to Kai-Fu Lee, an artificial intelligence expert who has worked for Google, Microsoft and Apple, China is increasingly active in the field of artificial intelligence (AI), and the U.S. should double the amount it spends on research.
Commentary comes after various parts of the U.S. government have released announcements about AI, even though the U.S. as a whole doesn't have any formal strategy for artificial intelligence.
In the meantime, China has launched a plan last year under which it wants to be number one in AI innovation by 2030.
“Dubbing the AI research budget would be a good start, given the fact that all other countries are farther away than the U.S. and we’re looking for another breakthrough in AI,” Lee said.
Speaking to CNBC this week, Lee said a doubling of funding could double the chance that the next major AI discovery will be made in the U.S.
Lee, who recently published Houghton Mifflin Harcourt at Houghton Mifflin Harcourt's book "Super superpower of AI: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order," is the CEO of Sinovation Ventures, which has invested in one of the most prominent AI companies in China, Face++.
