Researchers in the U.S. are often hard to try hard to receive government grants.
"Academia's leaders aren't done by China, but by corporations," Lee says.
Facebook, Google and other technology companies have been employing the best experts from universities in recent years to work on artificial intelligence.
Speaking of Immigration policy could also be helped by improvements in AI efforts, according to Lee.
"I think green cards should be automatically offered to holders of an AI doctorate," he says.
The State Council of China issued a New Generation Artificial Intelligence Plan in July 2017.
China's National Natural Sciences Foundation provides funds to people in academic institutions in a similar way as the National Scientific Council and other government organizations give money to researchers in the U.S, but the quality of academia's work is worse in China, Lee says.
The U.S. Department of Defense this year has set up a Joint Center for Artificial Intelligence to involve industry-aspiring and academia-envir-edged partners, the White House has announcing the creation of the Artificied Artificied Selection Commission.
And DARPA announced this month that it would invest $2 billion in an AI Next initiative.
When it comes to the NSF Foundation, it now invests more than $100 million a year in AI research.
