The Macedonians Vote in Referendum to Curse the Name of the Country
The people of Macedonia on Sunday voted in a referendum on whether the country's name should be changed to "Republions of North Macedonia", which would resolve a decades-old controversy with Greece, blocking applications for membership in the European Union and NATO.
Greece has a province-named Madedonia and argues that the name of the northern neighbor is a claim to its territory and has therefore vetoed Macedonia's entry into NATO and the EU.
The two governments entered into an agreement on the proposed new name in June, but nationalist opponents argue that the change will weaken the ethnic identity of the majority Slavic population in Macedonia.
President Gyoge Ivanov said he would not vote in the referendum, and a campaign calling for a boycott has raised doubts as to whether at least 50 per cent of the electorate would come forward in order for the referendum to be valid.
The question in the referendum was: "Are you in favor of membership in NATO and the EU with the adoption of an agreement with Greece?"
Supporters of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, say it's the price it's worth paying for Macedonia, one of the successor countries of the former Jugoslavia, to join organisations such as the EU-NATO.
“I’ve come today to vote for the future of the country, for young people in Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the roof of the European Union, because it means a safer life for all of us,” said 79-year-old Georgievska of Skopje.
Although the referendum is non-binding, enough members of the parliament have said they would follow the result of the vote.
Changing a name would require a two-thirds majority in parliament.
The State Electoral Commission said no problems had been reported within 13 a.m.
Electoral turnout, however, was just 16 per per cent, compared to 34 percent in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66 percent of registered voters eventually cast their vote.
“I went to vote because of my children, our place is in Europe,” says Gjose Tanevski, a 62-year-old Voter from Skopje.
Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and son Dushko are handing out their votes in a Macedonian referendum on a name change that would pave the country the way to NATO and the European Union in Strumica on September 30, 2018 on September 30, 2018.
In front of the Skopje parliament, Vladimir Kawardarkov is preparing a small podium and dragging chairs in front of tents built by people boycotting the referendum.
“We are in favor of NATO and the EU, but we want to come into them with our heads erected, not a door for the servants,” Kavadarkov said.
“We are a poor country, but we have our pride.
“If they don’t want us like Macedonia, we can turn to others, such as China and Russia, and become part of Eurasian integration.”
Prime Minister Zaev says NATO's membership will bring Macedonia, which has an unemployment rate of more than 20%, much-necessarily needed investment.
"I think a big majority will be in favour, because more than 80 per cent of our citizens are in favor of the EU and NATO," Zaev said after voting.
The “yes” result, according to him, would “conservation of our future.”
According to a poll last Monday published by the Macedonian Institute for Policy Research, the referendum could take part between 30 and 43 percent of the electorate – that is less than the required turnaround.
In another poll organized by the Macedonian station Telma TV, it has been found that 57 per cent of respondents intend to vote on Sunday.
Of these, 70 per cent said they would vote yes.
In order for the referendum to be successful, 50 per cent, plus a single voter, has to participate.
Failure of the referendum would mean the first serious blow to the policy of the pro-Western government since it started last May.
