The majority of those eight years I have been a local member of parliament on behalf of the Labour Party were exactly like that on Friday night.
In this day, unfortunately, most meetings do not take place, and the vow of a “kindergarier, kinder” politics has long since been forgotten, as long as it has ever started.
It is becoming increasingly apparent that different perspectives do not be tolerated in the Labour Party, and every opinion is being judged whether it is alibaciable for the leadership of the party.
Colleagues, whom I previously thought we had similar political views, suddenly expected me to turn 180-degree and hold positions that I could not otherwise agree with - whether it's national security or an EU-sponsible market.
At any time I speak in the public – and it doesn’t even matter what I’m saying – follows the tirades of social media swearing, calling for my dismissal, judging Wednesday’s policies and telling me I shouldn’t be in the Labour Party.
And it's not just my experience.
I'm learning that I'm more lucky than some of my colleagues, because the comments aimed at me are usually political.
I have the respect for the professionalism and determination of those, my colleagues who are facing a torrent of sexist or racist insults every day, but will never back down.
One of the aspects of this era of politics that I'm most disappointed about is how insults are becoming the norm.
Jeremy Corbyn stated last week that the Labour Party should cultivate a culture of tolerance.
Fact is such that we are no longer a diverse stream of opinion, and with every suggestion of disbeligament or a change in the rules of choice, the party becomes more limited.
Over the last two years, they've been advising me many times to stay back, he hasn't sounded so much, and then "I'm going to be cool."
But that's not what I went into politics for.
Situated by Margaret Thatcher’s negligence, who literally disintegrated my general school class, I’ve tried to fight for better public services for those most in need – whether as a local councillor or a government minister.
I've never hid my policy, even at the last election.
No one at Nottingham East could be surprised by my political positions and areas in which I do not agree with the current leadership.
To those who supported the proposal on Friday, I'd only say is that when a country is pushing for Brexit that harms households, businesses, businesses and our public services, I don't understand the desire to waste time and energy on my semi-alcoability towards the Labour leader.
Of course, my main message is not determined by Moment in Nottingham, but to my constituents, whether they are members of the Labour Party or not: I am proud to serve you, and I vow that no threat of removal or political pragmatism will discourage me from acting according to what I think is the best interests of all of you.
Chris Leslie is a member of parliament for Nottingham East
