'Sea is ours': landlocked Bolivia hopes the court reopens the path to the Pacific Ocean
Sailors patrol the naval headquarters in La Paz, which is decouplied by a cable car.
Above the public buildings there is a blue flag in the color of the ocean.
Naval bases from Lake Titicaca for the Amazon are described by the motto: "The sea is rightly our own.
Acquisition is our duty.”
There's still a vivid remembrance of the coast that the country has lost in favor of Chile in a bloody 19th-centicurse war - as well as a desire to re-emvite the Silent Ocean.
These aspirations are perhaps the largest in decades, because Bolivia is awaiting the decision of the International Court of Justice, which should fall on October 1 after five years of deliberation.
"Bolivia has the energy, the spirit of unity and calm and, of course, awaiting a positive result," Bolivian diplomat Roberto Calzadilla said.
Many Bolivians will follow MSD’s decisions on major screens across the country with the hope that the tribunal in The Hague will decide in favor of the Bolivian assertion that, after many decades of intermittent talks, Chile is obliged to act on Bolivia to provide a sovereign approach to the sea.
Charismatic Indigenous Indigenous President of Bolivia Evo Morales—who faces a controversial re-equip battle ahead of next year’s re-election.
"We're very close to going back to the Silent Ocean," he said at the end of August.
However, some analysts believe it is unlikely that the court will rule in favor of Bolivia – and even if that were to happen, not much would have changed.
This United Nations body, headquartered in the Netherlands, does not have the power to accuppose a Chilean territory, stating that it will not diagnose the outcome of possible talks.
The fact that MSD's decision came just six months after the latest arguments were made shows that the case "it wasn't complicated," as Paz Zarate, a Chilean expert on international law.
And for the last four years, not only did they not support Bolivia's cause, but maybe they've hindered her.
"Questions for access to the seas have seized the existing Bolivian government," Zarate says.
He adds that Morales' offensive rhetoric has weakened Chile's remaining good will.
Bolivia and Chile will one day begin to act again, but after this it will be extremely difficult to discuss.
Both countries haven't exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, Bolivia's representative in The Hague, dismissed the idea that the court's decision-making had been unreasonably rapid.
Monday will bring Bolivia an “exceptional opportunity to start a new era of ties with Chile” and the chance to “end 139 years of disagreements with mutual benefits,” he said.
Calzadilla has also dismissed Morales - still one of the most popular presidents in Latin America - to use the issue of the sea as a political crutch.
"Bolivia will never waive its right to have access to the Pacific Ocean," he added.
“The verdict is an opportunity to find out that we need to deal with the past.”
