John Edward: Linguistic Skills Are Necessitions for Global Citizens
Scotch-in-an independent schools have undergone a number of academic achievements, which continues in 2018 with other excellent test results. Individual and group achievements in sporting, artistic, music and other community activities only underscore this.
These schools have more than 30,000 pupils throughout Scotland, represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) and tries to provide their pupils and parents with the best possible level of service.
Independent schools prepare their pupils for further and higher education, the careers they have chosen and their place as global citizens.
As an educative sector that can design and implement individually tailored school educational plans, we observe that modern languages are still popular and desirable subjects in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you talk to a person in a language he understands, it goes straight into his head.
“If you talk to him in his own language, it goes right into his heart.”
It is a significant observation that if we want to build up relations and trust among people from other countries, we cannot rely only on English.
The last results of this year's renegotiations show that languages are in the first place of tables, while independent schools have the highest success rate.
A total of 68 per cent of pupils who learned a foreign language achieved the best A grade.
Data collected out of 74 SCIS member schools show that 72 percent of Mandarin students, 72 per cent of German students, 69 per cent French students, and 63 per cent of Spanish students.
This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland promote foreign languages as an important skills that children and teenagers will undoubtedly need in the future.
Languages such as subjects chosen by pupils are equally esteemed in the study plans of independent and other schools as STEM (knowledges, technologies, technical disciplines and maths).
A survey carried out by the British Commission for Employment and Skills in 2014 has shown that for the reasons that employers list as problems when filling vacancies, 17 per cent are inadequate language skills.
That's why language skills are becoming more and more necessary to prepare young people for their future careers.
Since more and more employment opportunities require languages, these skills are a necessity in a globalized world.
Whichever career a person chooses, if he has learned a second language, in the future he will have the real advantage that he has such a lifelong skill.
Thanks to the ability to directly communicate directly with people from foreign countries, a person talking multiple languages automatically gains an advantage over competition.
According to a poll conducted by YouGov in 2013 among 4,000 adults in the UK, 75 percent of them failed to speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation, the only language spoken by a double-digided percentage of people, specifically 15 percent, was French.
That's why it's an important thing for today's children to be invested in language learning.
If children will know more languages, especially the languages of emerging economies, they will have a better chance of finding a meaningful job.
In Scot, each school verses what languages it learns.
A number of schools focus on more classical modern languages, while others teach languages that are considered most important for the UK in the outlook for 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
No matter what the child is interested in, he will always have a choice of several languages in independent schools with teachers who are experts in the field.
Skate's independent schools are dedicated to ensuring an educational environment that will prepare and equip children with the skills needed for success, no matter in the future.
At this point, we cannot deny that languages are absolutely important for the future of the country in a global business environment, so it has to be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages should be regarded as “international communication skills.”
Independiant schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence for young people in Scotland.
Il faut bien le faire.
“John Edward Is Director of the Scottish Board of Independent Schools
