HIV/Aids: China Reports 14% Increase in New Cases
China reported a 14-percent surge in the number of citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
According to official figures, more than 820 000 people are affected by this virus.
Approximately 40 000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018.
The vast majority of new cases were transmitted by sex, which meant a change compared to the past.
Trareguously, HIV is circulating through some parts of China as a result of infected blood transfusions.
But the number of people infecting HIV in this way has been reduced to almost zero, Chinese health officials said at a conference in Yunnan province.
The number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China has risen by a year-on-year increase by 100 000 people.
Transmitting HIV through sex is an acute problem in the Chinese LGBT community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is reportedly still widespread.
Due to the conservative values of the country, the study estimates that 70-90% of men who have sex with men will eventually marry women.
Many of the transmissions of the disease come from the lack of sexual protection during these resemblies.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged a one-seater approach to HIV treatment as part of an effort to address the problem.
