The inventor of the World Wide Web site plans to start a new Internet and overtook Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, is launching a startup that seeks to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The newest technology legend project, Inrupt, is a company that builds on Berners-Lee's open source platform called Solid.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is stored and which people have access to the information.
Speaking with Fast Company Berners-Lee, he joked that the goal of the Inrupt project was "world domination."
"We've got to do it now," he said of the startup.
"It's a historic moment."
The application uses Solid technology, which allows people to create their own “Personal online data storage” or POD.
It can contain contact lists, to-do lists, a calendar, a music library and other personal and professional tools.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are available in the same browser and all at the same time at the same time.
What's exceptional about a personal online data storage is that it's entirely on the user to determine who will have access to what kind of piece of information.
The company denys this "personal authorization through data."
The Purpose of the Inrupt project, according to CEO of John Bruce, is to bring resources, processes and corresponding skills in order to help the Solid platform be made available to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by IBM, some employees who work on the project have collaborated on the project and community of voluntary programmers.
Starting this week, technologising developers around the world can create their own decentralized applications using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee has said that he and his team with "Facebook and Google don't talk about introducing a complete change where all their business models are inverted upside-down overnight.
"We don't ask for their permission."
Berners-Lee, in a media post published on Saturday, wrote that the Inrupt project's mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem that will help protect the integrity and quality of a new site built on the Solid platform.
Berners-Lee transformed the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in the discussion of network neutrality.
Berners-Lee will remain the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm unbelievably unparallapsible for this next era of the site," Berners-Lee added.
