“When you have so much experience at such a young age – he was at his first Olympics when he was 14 years old and his father died three years later of cancer – he said it wasn’t easy to find someone at the same age who had experienced similar outlences and downgrades.
In June, the pair adopted their son Robert Ray Black-Daley and Daley said it had "changed his entire point of view."
"Would you have asked me last year, it would have been "wanted to win a gold medal," he said.
"You know, there are bigger things than Olympic gold medals.
My Olympic medal is Robbie.”
His son was named after his father Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 after he was diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said that his father didn't accept that he was going to die, and the last thing he asked was if they already had tickets to the London Olympics in 2012 because he wanted to be in the front rows.
"I couldn't tell him - you won't be in the first place, Dad," he said.
"I held his hand when he stopped breathing, and it wasn't until the moment he stopped breathing and he was dead I finally realized he wasn't invincible," he said.
A year later Daley participated in the Olympics in 2012 and won bronze.
“I’ve known I’ve been dreaming about it all my life—jushing in front of our locals at the Olympics, I didn’t know a better feeling,” he said.
It also inspired the selection of his first composition – the Heather Small Proud – which resonated with him at the start of the Olympics and still evoked goosebumps.
