The National Trust listens to the secret life of bats
There's a new research going on at a bat housing estate in the Scottish highlands to uncover how bats use the landscape in food hunting.
There is a hope that research findings will shed a new light on the behavior of unique flying mammals and will help manage future activities to preserve this species.
The study, carried out by scientists from the National Trust for Scotland, will monitor bats whistling and the smallest even brown long and aquatic at the Inverewe Gardens botanical garden in Wester Ross.
In the key areas in this bat housing estate will place special recorders, which will monitor the activities of the bats throughout the time of the year.
NHS employees and volunteers will also conduct mobile reconnaissance using manual detectors.
An expert audio analysis of all recordings will determine the frequency of calls of bats and what the individual species do.
Subsequently, a map of the habitat and a report will be published, which will create a detailed landscape image of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, NTS conservation adviser, hopes the results will show which areas of the habitat are the most important for bats, and how different species use them.
This information will help to determine the advantages of habitat management work, like creating a meadow, and the best procedure for maintaining forested areas for bats and other related species.
Bat populations in Scotland and the United Kingdom in the last century are declining.
Threathed as a result of construction and territorial development, which affects colonies and the natural environment.
Wind turbines and lighting can also pose a risk, as well as flies traps and the chemical treatment of building materials as well as attacks of cats.
Bats aren't really glued.
Due to the fact that they hunt at night, however, their ears are more useless than the eyes.
They use an ingeniable echo-location technique to traime beetles and obstacles in flight.
NTS, accountable for the care of more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76 000 hectares of land in the country, takes bats very seriously.
He has trained experts who regularly conduct surveys, colony inspections and sometimes rescue work.
The organization even set up the first-and-a-single bat reservation at the Threave housing estate in Dumfries and Galloway, which is home to eight out of ten bat species in Scotland.
The manoeuvre of the housing estate David Thompson states that this is an ideal territory for them.
"We've got a great area here in Threave," he said.
"We've got old buildings, lots of ancient trees and the best habitat.
But there is still too much we don't know about bats, so the work we're doing here and in other places will help us to better understand what they need to survive."
It emphasizes the importance of control of bats before starting maintenance work in real estate, because it is possible that uneasy destruction of one maternity breeding site can kill up to 400 females and cubs, which can destroy an entire local population.
Bats are protected and it's illegal to kill, harass, disturb or destroy their colonies.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish Bat Conservation Trust official, encourages the public to put her hand over and help.
She said: "We still have a lot to learn about our bats and a lot that we don't know how their populations thrive."
