Why the Sino-E-U.S. Trade Wrought Could Be Good for Beijing
The introductory salvage of the U.S.-China trade war was decelerating, and although the battle is far from being beat-to-reached, according to experts, the fissure between countries in long-term consideration is advantageous for Beijing.
Donald Trump, the U.S. president, provided the first warning this year by taxing China's exports, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
However, most of the most important escalations saw the light of day this week, when new tariffs on $200 billion worth of goods were announced, which practically means the taxation of half of trading exports from China to the U.S.
Beijing has responded every time, most recently by imposing tariffs at five to 10 percent of U.S. $60-billion-dollar imports of U.S. goods.
China tried to repay the U.S. with the same coin and it is unlikely that the world's second-largest economy will knelt.
Even if China wants Washington to go back to its original tracks, it would mean giving way to its demands. But a public subjugation would be utterly humiliating for Chinese President Xi Jinping.
Even so, experts think that if Beijing were able to play its cards right, the U.S.-trafficus trade war could positively support China in a long-term way, as it would reduce the interdependencies of these two economies.
"Finitely, the rapid political decisions of Washington or Beijing could create economic conditions to trigger an economic collisible in both countries. That's far more dangerous than observers have previously admitted," Abigail Grace, a research collaborator who focuses on Asia at think tank Center for New American Security, said in a statement.
Syria Ready to Resume Refugees, Foreign Minister Says
Syria argues that it is ready for the voluntary recurrence of refugees and calls for help in the rebuilding of a country devastated by more than a seven-year-old war.
Speaking to the U.N. General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem said conditions in the country were improving.
"Today's situation on the territory is more stable and safer thanks to progress in the fight against terrorism," he said.
The government continues to rehabilitate areas devastated by terrorists into a normal state.
Now all the relief conditions for the voluntary return of refugees who have been compelled to leave the country as a result of terrorism, as well as the unilateral economic measures aimed at their day-to-day lives.
The UN estimates that more than 5.5 million people have left Syria since the war began in 2011.
Another six-million-strength population still lives in the country with the need for humanitarian aid.
Al-Moualem said the Syrian regime welcomes any assistance in building a devastated country.
However, he emphasized that he would not accept conditional assistance or assistance from countries that sponsored the rebels.
