Updating on red tide: Concentrations are declining in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota
Florida Fish and Wildlife's latest report points to an overall decrease in red tidal concentrations at Tampa Bay.
According to FWC reports, screes of blooming water are now reported from the districts of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier – this indicates a declining concentration.
The blooming red tide has a range of about 130 miles of coastline from Pinellas County to the southern district of Lee.
Spots have been observed about 10 miles from the coast in the Hillsborough district, but last week it was already relatively fewer places.
A red tide has also been observed in Pasco County.
Last week there were reported mean concentrations off the coast or on the high seas near Pinellas County. In Hillsborough County, low to high concentrations were observed on the high seas, the lowest up to high concentrations in Manatee, the lowest up to high concentrations off the coast or on the high seas near Sarasota County, the lowest-to-central concentration in the Charlotte district, the lowest up to high concentrations off the coast or on high seas near the Lee district and low concentrations.
Respiratory irritories continue to occur in the districts of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
Irritocation of the airwave has not been reported in north-Western Florida last week.
